242
00:10:46,833 --> 00:10:48,900
"You see, when a man 
loves a car very much,

243
00:10:48,900 --> 00:10:51,267
you recline the seat back, 
you take the car--"

244
00:10:51,267 --> 00:10:53,167
 Now, these are accident reports 
 from my headquarters.

245
00:10:53,167 --> 00:10:54,500
 "Officer, is this gonna take long?

246
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:56,667
Each figure represents 
someone injured or killed...

247
00:10:56,667 --> 00:10:58,767
 "We got a crop out in the field 
 we got to cut--

248
00:10:58,767 --> 00:11:01,300
- on city streets, farm roads, 
- "It's getting late.

249
00:11:01,300 --> 00:11:02,833
highways and railway crossings.

250
00:11:02,833 --> 00:11:04,767
"We've got to mow that hay. 
Got to get it to market.

251
00:11:04,767 --> 00:11:05,967
All the facts 
and figures are here.

252
00:11:05,967 --> 00:11:07,233
We lose money if we don't."

253
00:11:07,233 --> 00:11:08,733
Some of them you've 
read about in the papers.

254
00:11:08,733 --> 00:11:10,467
"Others we hushed up."

255
00:11:10,467 --> 00:11:12,300
Big spectacular crashes

256
00:11:12,300 --> 00:11:15,433
- caused by terrific speed 
- Oh. Mm.

257
00:11:15,433 --> 00:11:18,167
or drunk driving 
or some other form

258
00:11:18,167 --> 00:11:20,033
of what I call near-insanity.

259
00:11:20,033 --> 00:11:22,233
Stop the near-insanity!

260
00:11:22,233 --> 00:11:23,733
But most people only 
squeeze the law a little--

261
00:11:23,733 --> 00:11:24,767
( siren wailing )

262
00:11:24,767 --> 00:11:27,267
at stop signs, 
in restricted speed zones,

263
00:11:27,267 --> 00:11:28,667
and the like.

264
00:11:28,667 --> 00:11:31,333
Little violations that 
most people get away with

265
00:11:31,333 --> 00:11:35,567
time after time, 
maybe getting a ticket

266
00:11:35,567 --> 00:11:38,567
 - or a warning, like Frank, Jr. did, 
 - "Iowa P.D. Blue."

267
00:11:38,567 --> 00:11:40,100
but without getting hurt.

268
00:11:40,100 --> 00:11:42,300
But those little violations,

269
00:11:42,300 --> 00:11:44,233
Ooh, wow!

270
00:11:44,233 --> 00:11:46,700
those little chances 
you take day by day,

271
00:11:46,700 --> 00:11:49,733
 that's where you start 
 playing roulette with your life.

272
00:11:49,733 --> 00:11:52,333
You're taking a big gamble 
with your life

273
00:11:52,333 --> 00:11:53,600
( groaning )

274
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:56,067
when you violate a no passing 
line on the road.

275
00:11:57,100 --> 00:11:58,133
Whoa!

276
00:11:58,133 --> 00:12:00,333
"My earlobes 
won't stop growing."

277
00:12:00,333 --> 00:12:04,267
You're taking a big chance 
on the car in front

278
00:12:04,267 --> 00:12:07,667
when you follow too 
closely at highway speeds.

279
00:12:07,667 --> 00:12:10,833
 Maybe a man will do these things 
 a hundred times

280
00:12:10,833 --> 00:12:13,867
- in perfect safety-- 
- Oh, the odds are pretty good.

281
00:12:13,867 --> 00:12:15,833
squeeze the law-- 
and nothing happens.

282
00:12:15,833 --> 00:12:18,333
 But don't forget that other law,

283
00:12:18,333 --> 00:12:21,267
- the law of averages. 
- "I regret nothing."

284
00:12:21,267 --> 00:12:24,800
Sooner or later, 
it catches up with you.

285
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:26,133
Oh.

286
00:12:26,133 --> 00:12:27,733
Now, this accident

287
00:12:27,733 --> 00:12:29,900
was number one 
for the driver involved.

288
00:12:29,900 --> 00:12:32,100
The first one he'd ever had.

289
00:12:32,100 --> 00:12:34,567
But it was his last one, too.

290
00:12:34,567 --> 00:12:36,967
He took one little chance 
too many.

291
00:12:36,967 --> 00:12:40,167
Mother: Frank is taking 
too many chances in his car.

292
00:12:40,167 --> 00:12:41,200
"Mother, no."

293
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:43,400
Oh, he'll be all right. 
Won't he, Hal?

294
00:12:43,400 --> 00:12:45,300
 He'll be all right if he takes to heart

295
00:12:45,300 --> 00:12:47,633
what I told him about signs.

296
00:12:47,633 --> 00:12:50,500
"Signs of Life"--

297
00:12:50,500 --> 00:12:53,733
 That's what stop signs and all 
 the other road signs really are.

298
00:12:53,733 --> 00:12:54,867
Mm-hmm.

299
00:12:54,867 --> 00:12:56,567
And missing one of them 
as you drive along

300
00:12:56,567 --> 00:12:59,133
- or just ignore it, 
- "Anybody know this guy?"

301
00:12:59,133 --> 00:13:01,200
 that's one of those little things 
 that kills people.

302
00:13:01,200 --> 00:13:02,700
"The world made sense 
an hour ago."

303
00:13:02,700 --> 00:13:04,233
Whenever there's 
a hazard on the road,

304
00:13:04,233 --> 00:13:06,767
there's usually a sign 
to tell you about it,

305
00:13:06,767 --> 00:13:09,200
- like a curve warning 
- ( humming tune )

306
00:13:09,200 --> 00:13:12,267
or a right of way sign, 
narrow bridge,

307
00:13:12,267 --> 00:13:14,333
- signals ahead, do not enter, 
- I see. Mm-hmm.

308
00:13:14,333 --> 00:13:16,500
- Doesn't mean a thing. 
- no passing, and many others.

309
00:13:16,500 --> 00:13:19,667
- "All-nude girls." 
- "Whites only."

310
00:13:19,667 --> 00:13:22,433
But for too many drivers 
a stop sign,

311
00:13:22,433 --> 00:13:24,567
- like this, for example, 
- Is a target.

312
00:13:24,567 --> 00:13:26,867
has no importance 
unless they can see cars

313
00:13:26,867 --> 00:13:29,900
crossing the road ahead 
right now.

314
00:13:29,900 --> 00:13:33,800
A school crossing sign 
for some drivers

315
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:35,767
seems to mean little or nothing

316
00:13:35,767 --> 00:13:38,633
- ( imitating ducklings ) 
- unless there are children

317
00:13:38,633 --> 00:13:41,733
actually crossing the street 
at that moment.

318
00:13:41,733 --> 00:13:44,267
- As for railroad crossings, 
- Forget 'em.

319
00:13:44,267 --> 00:13:45,800
- ( train horn blows ) 
- some drivers act as if

320
00:13:45,800 --> 00:13:49,767
the warning signs and tracks 
don't even exist

321
00:13:49,767 --> 00:13:51,433
unless there's a streamliner

322
00:13:51,433 --> 00:13:53,567
 passing by right in front of them.

323
00:13:53,567 --> 00:13:55,833
Dirty Mary and Crazy Larry, no!

324
00:13:55,833 --> 00:13:57,833
Hard as it is to believe,

325
00:13:57,833 --> 00:13:59,733
there are many accidents 
where the driver

326
00:13:59,733 --> 00:14:01,567
ran past three warning signs

327
00:14:01,567 --> 00:14:03,933
and straight into the side 
of a moving train.

328
00:14:03,933 --> 00:14:05,800
Ah, good riddance, I say.

329
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:07,933
 ...broad daylight, but it happens

330
00:14:07,933 --> 00:14:09,767
even more often at night.

331
00:14:09,767 --> 00:14:11,900
 - That's when the warning sign, 
 - ( growls )

332
00:14:11,900 --> 00:14:14,433
the signal, the crossbuck,

333
00:14:14,433 --> 00:14:16,700
are really your signs of life.

334
00:14:16,700 --> 00:14:19,600
"You see, son, 
we all die alone and afraid."

335
00:14:19,600 --> 00:14:20,667
The sign is still there,

336
00:14:20,667 --> 00:14:23,400
but you have to be twice 
as alert to see it.

337
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:26,633
 - If you hadn't seen the signs, 
 - Boy, you haven't lived.

338
00:14:26,633 --> 00:14:29,000
this train might come 
as a surprise to you.

339
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,500
"The Sou-oul Train!"

340
00:14:31,500 --> 00:14:33,167
( deep voice ) 
Thank you very much, Clarissa.

341
00:14:35,333 --> 00:14:38,500
- Anytown, U.S.A.! 
- Traffic signs and signals

342
00:14:38,500 --> 00:14:40,933
that are so easy 
to see in the daylight

343
00:14:40,933 --> 00:14:44,333
become easier to miss 
as the sun goes down.

344
00:14:44,333 --> 00:14:47,633
 Twilight is a dangerous time of day

345
00:14:47,633 --> 00:14:49,833
when things are half visible,

346
00:14:49,833 --> 00:14:52,500
- half in deepening shadow. 
- "Half-naked."

347
00:14:52,500 --> 00:14:55,100
 Main Street at night becomes a maze

348
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:57,700
of lights and neon signs,

349
00:14:57,700 --> 00:15:00,267
all competing with one another 
to catch your eye.

350
00:15:00,267 --> 00:15:02,100
So don't ever go to the city. 
It's dirty.

351
00:15:02,100 --> 00:15:04,000
 - But where's the traffic signal? 
 - In your pants.

352
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:05,867
Oh, there it is,

353
00:15:05,867 --> 00:15:08,433
almost lost against 
the other lights beyond.

354
00:15:08,433 --> 00:15:10,867
God, I had some times 
in this town. Anyway...

355
00:15:10,867 --> 00:15:13,700
Even the blinking red light 
of the railroad wigwag

356
00:15:13,700 --> 00:15:15,267
is hard to pick out sometimes.

357
00:15:15,267 --> 00:15:17,433
 Spilled my youth like cheap gin. 
 Anyway...

358
00:15:17,433 --> 00:15:20,300
But night or day, 
these are your signs of life,

359
00:15:20,300 --> 00:15:23,333
 whose purpose is to keep you alive.

360
00:15:23,333 --> 00:15:26,533
Even though you 
don't deserve it.

361
00:15:26,533 --> 00:15:29,500
It's a strange thing, 
but some people actually resent

362
00:15:29,500 --> 00:15:33,700
 - traffic signs and traffic laws. 
 - "We resent you being here."

363
00:15:33,700 --> 00:15:35,533
Cramps their style.

364
00:15:35,533 --> 00:15:37,933
They figure rules 
are for the other fellow,

365
00:15:37,933 --> 00:15:40,033
not for good drivers 
like themselves.

366
00:15:40,033 --> 00:15:42,867
- They're communists. 
- Here's one like that.

367
00:15:42,867 --> 00:15:44,900
Robert Bainbridge.

368
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:47,967
Bainbridge? 
From Meridian High?

369
00:15:47,967 --> 00:15:51,467
 Oh, I know him. I ran against him 
 on the track team.

370
00:15:51,467 --> 00:15:53,633
"Well, do you know him 
like this?"

371
00:15:53,633 --> 00:15:55,100
"No, no! 
( screams )

372
00:15:55,100 --> 00:15:59,333
"Dear God, who are you? 
What are you?"

373
00:16:01,533 --> 00:16:04,533
"I'm gonna go throw myself 
in the thresher."

374
00:16:04,533 --> 00:16:06,067
( sobbing )

375
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:10,100
I'm sorry, dear.

376
00:16:10,100 --> 00:16:12,300
♪♪

377
00:16:17,033 --> 00:16:18,967
- Yeah. 
- Even with sensible,

378
00:16:18,967 --> 00:16:20,900
safe-driving people 
on a highway as smooth as silk,

379
00:16:20,900 --> 00:16:22,833
♪ Kill the wabbit, 
kill the wabbit ♪

380
00:16:22,833 --> 00:16:25,333
with no traffic lights 
and intersections,

381
00:16:25,333 --> 00:16:28,533
- Wow! 
- you might wonder,

382
00:16:28,533 --> 00:16:31,833
"Where is the danger 
we keep talking about?"

383
00:16:31,833 --> 00:16:34,533
 - Well, here's one danger-- fatigue. 
 - Kidnapping.

384
00:16:34,533 --> 00:16:38,033
On long trips, 
fatigue is a big hazard.

385
00:16:38,033 --> 00:16:42,200
You should stop 
at least once every hour.

386
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:45,067
- Get out, stretch your legs, 
- Take some bennies.

387
00:16:45,067 --> 00:16:47,867
 - get those cobwebs out of your head. 
 - ( snorts, mutters )

388
00:16:47,867 --> 00:16:50,867
Weariness slows down 
your reactions,

389
00:16:50,867 --> 00:16:53,067
and at 60 miles an hour 
or better,

390
00:16:53,067 --> 00:16:56,800
- you can't afford it. 
- ( snoring, muttering )

391
00:16:58,367 --> 00:17:00,767
"My life is a lie!" 
Oh, no.

392
00:17:00,767 --> 00:17:03,200
"Mm, don't love you, Martha." 
( muttering )

393
00:17:06,300 --> 00:17:08,033
- ( gasps ) 
- Whoops.

394
00:17:08,033 --> 00:17:10,067
Now they're Ma and Pa Topper.

395
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:13,933
 And here is still another danger--

396
00:17:13,933 --> 00:17:15,833
lack of attention to the road.

397
00:17:15,833 --> 00:17:18,833
Sure, it's an open highway. 
Maybe there aren't many cars.

398
00:17:18,833 --> 00:17:20,500
"Yeah, and I buried 
the victims right over there.

399
00:17:20,500 --> 00:17:23,733
 - But it only takes two to tango. 
 - Right there."

400
00:17:23,733 --> 00:17:25,967
Forty percent of all 
expressway accidents

401
00:17:25,967 --> 00:17:27,033
( imitates engine sputtering )

402
00:17:27,033 --> 00:17:28,767
involve a car that has 
already stopped--

403
00:17:28,767 --> 00:17:29,867
Driven by this guy.

404
00:17:29,867 --> 00:17:34,000
on the road, partway on, 
or not far enough off.

405
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,667
- This is an invitation... 
- "Look at that over there.

406
00:17:36,667 --> 00:17:38,167
 There's more of a... that thing."

407
00:17:38,167 --> 00:17:40,433
( tires screeching )

408
00:17:40,433 --> 00:17:41,833
( imitating Henry Kissinger ) 
Ach, Mr. President,

409
00:17:41,833 --> 00:17:43,467
 please watch where you're going.

410
00:17:43,467 --> 00:17:46,133
 Whoa! Almost ran over Kissinger.

411
00:17:46,133 --> 00:17:47,767
I'm gonna have to go back.

412
00:17:49,333 --> 00:17:51,833
Here is another hazard 
of our superhighways

413
00:17:51,833 --> 00:17:53,833
that we patrolmen see 
all too often.

414
00:17:53,833 --> 00:17:56,100
Women drivers.

415
00:17:56,100 --> 00:17:58,333
Every time you have to 
hit your brakes hard,

416
00:17:58,333 --> 00:18:00,367
Saints cry.

417
00:18:00,367 --> 00:18:03,100
ask yourself, 
why weren't you ready

418
00:18:03,100 --> 00:18:04,833
for the danger that 
suddenly developed?

419
00:18:04,833 --> 00:18:08,633
Forty percent of all accidents 
are caused by women's hinders.

420
00:18:08,633 --> 00:18:09,667
( trills tongue )

421
00:18:09,667 --> 00:18:11,500
The first car suddenly 
pulled out without looking.

422
00:18:11,500 --> 00:18:13,800
It's always sudden, of course,

423
00:18:13,800 --> 00:18:16,567
- and always surprising. 
- But never dull.

424
00:18:16,567 --> 00:18:18,667
A man who decides 
at the last moment...

425
00:18:18,667 --> 00:18:20,633
Grandma, no!

426
00:18:22,367 --> 00:18:25,933
The entering car that fails 
to yield the right of way.

427
00:18:25,933 --> 00:18:29,133
The paid assassin 
cruising through town.

428
00:18:29,133 --> 00:18:32,000
Well, sure, 
the other fellow's often wrong,

429
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:35,133
and if he causes a crash, 
it's all his fault.

430
00:18:35,133 --> 00:18:36,733
But you may be dead 
just the same.

431
00:18:36,733 --> 00:18:38,567
Would you please, please leave?

432
00:18:38,567 --> 00:18:40,467
In a lot of accidents, 
it's pretty hard to tell

433
00:18:40,467 --> 00:18:41,867
just who is to blame, 
isn't it, Hal?

434
00:18:41,867 --> 00:18:44,367
 - I ask you to talk? 
 - It's impossible to tell sometimes.

435
00:18:44,367 --> 00:18:46,633
I want my din-din!

436
00:18:46,633 --> 00:18:48,667
 - ...where there's never a single doubt. 
 - What's that?

437
00:18:48,667 --> 00:18:50,300
- At a railroad crossing. 
- Yep.

438
00:18:50,300 --> 00:18:52,500
 - The train sure has the right of way. 
 - Yeah, whatever.

439
00:18:52,500 --> 00:18:56,367
The railroad's been here 
for almost four generations.

440
00:18:56,367 --> 00:18:58,700
 Every farm family 
 in these parts uses the railroad

441
00:18:58,700 --> 00:19:00,600
to ship their crops.

442
00:19:00,600 --> 00:19:03,567
 - We all like to ride the train. 
 - I like to pretend I'm an engineer.

443
00:19:03,567 --> 00:19:07,400
 But why is it so many people 
 don't see the trains at the crossings?

444
00:19:07,400 --> 00:19:09,467
It seems impossible that anyone

445
00:19:09,467 --> 00:19:11,833
could fail to see 
or hear a train in motion.

446
00:19:11,833 --> 00:19:12,867
What train?

447
00:19:12,867 --> 00:19:15,667
A locomotive is certainly 
big enough,

448
00:19:15,667 --> 00:19:17,767
and the train of cars behind it

449
00:19:17,767 --> 00:19:20,467
may be as much as a mile long.

450
00:19:20,467 --> 00:19:22,600
 And yet, many people who have lived

451
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:25,133
 - through a crossing accident will say 
 - Aah!

452
00:19:25,133 --> 00:19:27,367
 - "Where did the train come from? 
 - That's what they'll say.

453
00:19:27,367 --> 00:19:30,833
 "I never saw it 
 until it was right on top of me."

454
00:19:30,833 --> 00:19:33,200
- Where did it come from? 
- None of your beeswax.

455
00:19:33,200 --> 00:19:35,200
Well, it came 
on the rails.

456
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:36,767
It couldn't come 
from anywhere else.

457
00:19:36,767 --> 00:19:37,967
Duh.

458
00:19:37,967 --> 00:19:40,133
But if you paid 
no attention to the signs,

459
00:19:40,133 --> 00:19:41,167
I hear it.

460
00:19:41,167 --> 00:19:44,567
 - didn't look and didn't listen, 
 - Then you deserve to die.

461
00:19:44,567 --> 00:19:47,100
then for you 
it came out of nowhere.

462
00:19:47,100 --> 00:19:49,767
An approaching train 
starts the signal

463
00:19:49,767 --> 00:19:52,767
while it is still 
some distance away.

464
00:19:52,767 --> 00:19:55,533
 Never try to guess how far away.

465
00:19:55,533 --> 00:19:57,967
Don't guess at all.

466
00:19:57,967 --> 00:20:01,133
 - And don't be impatient. 
 - And don't get a haircut like that.

467
00:20:01,133 --> 00:20:04,000
Because the train's coming, 
and it can't stop.

468
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
Trains are blameless, 
holy creatures.

469
00:20:11,033 --> 00:20:14,133
 - "I have no feelings for you!" 
 - "What?"

470
00:20:14,133 --> 00:20:16,600
 "This is a loveless relationship! 
 I want out!"

471
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:18,200
- "What?" 
- "I'm repulsed by your touch!"

472
00:20:18,200 --> 00:20:21,367
 - "What?" 
 - "Let me out right here! I hate you!"

473
00:20:21,367 --> 00:20:25,400
- ( imitating crossing bell ) 
- A signal in action means that

474
00:20:25,400 --> 00:20:27,833
 a condition of fatal danger exists

475
00:20:27,833 --> 00:20:30,767
 at this moment at this crossing.

476
00:20:30,767 --> 00:20:35,133
 Maybe the last train you waited for 
 was a slow freight

477
00:20:35,133 --> 00:20:37,567
that took several minutes 
to cross.

478
00:20:37,567 --> 00:20:39,733
But the next one 
may be a fast freight,

479
00:20:39,733 --> 00:20:42,267
high-balling to the coast 
on a tight schedule.

480
00:20:42,267 --> 00:20:46,833
It has no time to stop for you 
corn-shucking crackers.

481
00:20:46,833 --> 00:20:48,833
Remember that many 
freight trains today

482
00:20:48,833 --> 00:20:51,567
 are faster than passenger trains 
 of 15 years ago.

483
00:20:51,567 --> 00:20:53,233
Oh, wow!

484
00:20:53,233 --> 00:20:54,933
Many crossings have more 
than one set of tracks,

485
00:20:54,933 --> 00:20:57,467
each one a separate hazard.

486
00:20:57,467 --> 00:21:00,067
- When you cross, 
- Floss.

487
00:21:00,067 --> 00:21:02,067
make sure all tracks are clear.

488
