01:03:01,667 --> 01:03:03,200
They'll be very leery 
about moving

1458
01:03:03,200 --> 01:03:04,767
any of that 
material after this.

1459
01:03:04,767 --> 01:03:07,467
I don't think I'm gonna 
wait for them to move.

1460
01:03:09,800 --> 01:03:12,433
They called it Moby Car.

1461
01:03:12,433 --> 01:03:15,167
"I see the belly 
of the great white car."

1462
01:03:15,167 --> 01:03:17,867
- Thanks for the lift. 
- Is Moran up there?

1463
01:03:17,867 --> 01:03:20,033
 I don't know, 
 but I'm supposed to meet him here.

1464
01:03:20,033 --> 01:03:22,867
"I resent you. Good night."

1465
01:03:22,867 --> 01:03:24,867
"Man, I'm chafing like a bear.

1466
01:03:24,867 --> 01:03:27,567
These Italian bun-huggers 
give me no room at all."

1467
01:03:33,467 --> 01:03:36,067
"Oh, message for me. 
'Top boss needs to talk...'

1468
01:03:36,067 --> 01:03:38,067
I never got this message!"

1469
01:03:41,167 --> 01:03:42,700
Come in. I want to talk to you.

1470
01:03:42,700 --> 01:03:44,733
"Come in? 
How much more in could I be?"

1471
01:03:47,733 --> 01:03:49,400
I don't remember having 
a date with you.

1472
01:03:49,400 --> 01:03:50,667
"I must've been drunk."

1473
01:03:50,667 --> 01:03:52,700
You will.

1474
01:03:52,700 --> 01:03:55,033
- How'd you get in here? 
- Same way you did.

1475
01:03:55,033 --> 01:03:57,200
- "Levitation." 
- Well, what do you want?

1476
01:03:57,200 --> 01:03:58,367
"Same thing you want."

1477
01:03:58,367 --> 01:04:00,600
I'd like to ask you 
a couple of questions.

1478
01:04:00,600 --> 01:04:02,967
It's about that car 
that was abandoned awhile back.

1479
01:04:02,967 --> 01:04:05,033
What's that got to do with me?

1480
01:04:05,033 --> 01:04:07,067
You look like one of the boys 
that got away.

1481
01:04:07,067 --> 01:04:09,600
- Lots of guys look like me. 
- "You should see my mother."

1482
01:04:09,600 --> 01:04:11,900
Sorry. You'll have to 
try a new attack.

1483
01:04:11,900 --> 01:04:14,833
That's a good suggestion. 
Let's go down to headquarters.

1484
01:04:14,833 --> 01:04:16,833
It's a bum rap, copper.

1485
01:04:16,833 --> 01:04:19,367
 "I need a bigger steering wheel."

1486
01:04:21,167 --> 01:04:23,100
- Mickey. 
- "I like your cakes.

1487
01:04:23,100 --> 01:04:26,400
I mean, your desserts. 
Oh, never mind."

1488
01:04:26,400 --> 01:04:28,567
 - "Here. I've got your coleslaw." 
 - What are you doing here?

1489
01:04:28,567 --> 01:04:30,933
- I just drove up with Benson. 
- Is he up there?

1490
01:04:30,933 --> 01:04:33,300
Yeah. How about Blackie?

1491
01:04:33,300 --> 01:04:34,833
Now, how about him?

1492
01:04:34,833 --> 01:04:36,933
"Oh, outwitted again."

1493
01:04:36,933 --> 01:04:39,267
♪♪

1494
01:04:44,333 --> 01:04:46,467
Mind if I call my attorney?

1495
01:04:46,467 --> 01:04:48,067
Sure. Go right ahead.

1496
01:04:48,067 --> 01:04:49,900
"Hello. Law offices?"

1497
01:04:52,833 --> 01:04:54,933
- "I'm dull." 
- "No, I'm way duller."

1498
01:04:54,933 --> 01:04:56,767
"Okay, name my character."

1499
01:04:56,767 --> 01:04:58,200
"Uh, jeez. Uh, uh-- I can't!"

1500
01:04:58,200 --> 01:05:00,467
"See? Which one of us 
is the good guy?"

1501
01:05:00,467 --> 01:05:03,067
"I don't know, 
I don't know. Who am I?"

1502
01:05:04,800 --> 01:05:07,467
Ah, the Martin and Lewis 
breakup was so sad.

1503
01:05:07,467 --> 01:05:09,600
( imitating Jerry Lewis ) 
"Oh, with the punch and the..."

1504
01:05:09,600 --> 01:05:11,267
"Take it there."

1505
01:05:15,167 --> 01:05:16,667
"Sparky?

1506
01:05:16,667 --> 01:05:19,300
Sparky, bad dog! No!"

1507
01:05:21,100 --> 01:05:23,533
He was checking up 
on that car we ditched.

1508
01:05:23,533 --> 01:05:25,267
 They sure didn't waste any time.

1509
01:05:25,267 --> 01:05:27,000
"My gun. Gimme. Mine."

1510
01:05:27,000 --> 01:05:29,367
 Not here. 
 We'll take him down the back way.

1511
01:05:32,000 --> 01:05:34,533
"Okay, let's do that 
'Weekend at Bernie's' routine."

1512
01:05:34,533 --> 01:05:36,733
- ( footsteps ) 
- Let's get out of here.

1513
01:05:42,000 --> 01:05:44,500
♪ Frere Jacques dormez-- ♪ 
Merde!

1514
01:05:44,500 --> 01:05:47,667
( screaming ) 
Oh, there's a dead man!

1515
01:05:47,667 --> 01:05:49,000
It's all right, Myrtle. 
I'll take care of it.

1516
01:05:49,000 --> 01:05:51,533
Thank you!

1517
01:05:51,533 --> 01:05:53,500
The director's mistress, 
ladies and gentlemen.

1518
01:05:53,500 --> 01:05:56,033
The director's mistress.

1519
01:05:56,033 --> 01:05:57,500
Oh, good guy, bad guy?

1520
01:05:57,500 --> 01:06:00,433
Good guy or bad guy? 
I don't... which...

1521
01:06:00,433 --> 01:06:03,433
"Don't say it. 
I know. I'm blurry."

1522
01:06:07,800 --> 01:06:10,767
( groaning )

1523
01:06:10,767 --> 01:06:13,100
"Hey, where are my fries?"

1524
01:06:20,367 --> 01:06:22,667
 He must've got a bad clown burger.

1525
01:06:26,167 --> 01:06:27,800
What'd you use, a sledgehammer?

1526
01:06:27,800 --> 01:06:30,967
Hey, wait a minute. 
I didn't do it.

1527
01:06:30,967 --> 01:06:33,100
 No, I don't think you're the type.

1528
01:06:33,100 --> 01:06:34,200
Anyway, if you had,

1529
01:06:34,200 --> 01:06:36,000
you wouldn't have stuck around 
to play nursemaid.

1530
01:06:36,000 --> 01:06:37,900
You hurt bad?

1531
01:06:37,900 --> 01:06:40,700
- I'll live. 
- He's demonic.

1532
01:06:40,700 --> 01:06:43,500
 Hey, wait a minute. 
 You're Blackie's girl, aren't you?

1533
01:06:43,500 --> 01:06:45,033
That's no crime.

1534
01:06:45,033 --> 01:06:48,033
 - No. 
 - "Not in a strictly Cartesian sense."

1535
01:06:48,033 --> 01:06:49,167
It's a crime the way 
your friends

1536
01:06:49,167 --> 01:06:51,100
are letting him 
take the rap for them.

1537
01:06:51,100 --> 01:06:53,100
I don't know what 
you're talking about.

1538
01:06:53,100 --> 01:06:54,833
- We don't either. 
- Put it this way.

1539
01:06:54,833 --> 01:06:55,867
With Blackie in jail,

1540
01:06:55,867 --> 01:06:57,600
they don't have to 
cut him in on the payoff.

1541
01:06:57,600 --> 01:07:00,100
You don't call that 
playing it smart, do you?

1542
01:07:00,100 --> 01:07:01,833
What do you want me 
to do about it?

1543
01:07:01,833 --> 01:07:04,800
"Dress as a leopard 
and sing 'La traviata'."

1544
01:07:04,800 --> 01:07:08,000
 You help us, we'll help Blackie.

1545
01:07:08,000 --> 01:07:10,000
Tell us who's behind this.

1546
01:07:10,000 --> 01:07:11,900
"You scratch my head, 
I'll wash yours."

1547
01:07:11,900 --> 01:07:14,400
You mean, if I talk, 
you'll release him?

1548
01:07:14,400 --> 01:07:17,600
I can't make any promises, 
but we'll do all we can.

1549
01:07:17,600 --> 01:07:21,300
"I'll have to run it 
by the sales manager."

1550
01:07:21,300 --> 01:07:23,700
 Look, if you think your friends 
 are gonna be bighearted

1551
01:07:23,700 --> 01:07:25,867
and help Blackie, 
you're wasting your time.

1552
01:07:25,867 --> 01:07:28,667
Let me think it over, I said.

1553
01:07:28,667 --> 01:07:31,100
 Then, just when I thought 
 I had the situation under control,

1554
01:07:31,100 --> 01:07:32,867
- the roof fell in. 
- "'Boom,' it went."

1555
01:07:32,867 --> 01:07:35,400
 When I came to, there was 
 Blackie's girlfriend looking at me.

1556
01:07:35,400 --> 01:07:36,933
What a wonderful way 
to wake up.

1557
01:07:36,933 --> 01:07:38,133
Shut up.

1558
01:07:38,133 --> 01:07:39,733
I tried to get her to talk,

1559
01:07:39,733 --> 01:07:42,267
even promised to go easy 
on Blackie if she did.

1560
01:07:42,267 --> 01:07:44,800
No soap. I think 
she's softening, though.

1561
01:07:44,800 --> 01:07:46,333
I hope so, for her sake.

1562
01:07:48,633 --> 01:07:50,600
"Hi, sugar lumps."

1563
01:07:50,600 --> 01:07:52,733
"Hi, lamby legs."

1564
01:07:54,367 --> 01:07:56,433
 Wasn't such a good idea after all.

1565
01:07:56,433 --> 01:07:58,433
It's too bad they didn't get 
Mickey, too.

1566
01:07:58,433 --> 01:08:00,367
- Has he been around? 
- Not yet.

1567
01:08:00,367 --> 01:08:01,500
"Good Jumble today."

1568
01:08:01,500 --> 01:08:04,100
I'm getting a little jittery 
about the whole thing myself.

1569
01:08:04,100 --> 01:08:05,633
They seem to know 
every move we make.

1570
01:08:05,633 --> 01:08:06,933
( gasping ) Radar!

1571
01:08:06,933 --> 01:08:08,067
Do you know where 
to reach Moran?

1572
01:08:08,067 --> 01:08:09,533
He must be at the barn.

1573
01:08:09,533 --> 01:08:11,800
Get him on the phone 
and have him come over.

1574
01:08:11,800 --> 01:08:13,933
 I've got an idea I think might work.

1575
01:08:15,833 --> 01:08:18,800
 I wonder if she ever 
 talks into the statue by accident.

1576
01:08:20,633 --> 01:08:22,400
Why don't you shut up? 
I'm sick of listening to you.

1577
01:08:22,400 --> 01:08:23,733
Always complaining.

1578
01:08:23,733 --> 01:08:26,000
Why, you're probably 
healthier than I am.

1579
01:08:26,000 --> 01:08:28,333
I can't stand it anymore. 
This is the last straw.

1580
01:08:28,333 --> 01:08:29,367
This joint is killing me.

1581
01:08:29,367 --> 01:08:31,200
I'm getting out. 
I'm leaving...

1582
01:08:31,200 --> 01:08:34,700
- to get a drink of water. 
- ( weak chuckling )

1583
01:08:34,700 --> 01:08:36,767
Thank you, Sid.

1584
01:08:36,767 --> 01:08:38,400
Was it anything important?

1585
01:08:38,400 --> 01:08:41,400
 Lila wants to talk over something 
 lover boy's dreamed up.

1586
01:08:41,400 --> 01:08:43,200
Why don't we turn over 
the stuff to him right here

1587
01:08:43,200 --> 01:08:44,433
and get the rest of our dough?

1588
01:08:44,433 --> 01:08:46,133
The longer we hold on to it, 
the hotter it gets.

1589
01:08:46,133 --> 01:08:48,400
 I tried to sell him on that idea, 
 but he won't hold still.

1590
01:08:48,400 --> 01:08:50,000
 He wants to load it on the yacht.

1591
01:08:50,000 --> 01:08:51,667
 You don't suppose he's trying 
 to pull a fast one on us, do you?

1592
01:08:51,667 --> 01:08:53,800
Hey, that's it. He might try 
to double-cross us, huh?

1593
01:08:53,800 --> 01:08:55,633
- Keep your shirt on. 
- Yeah, please.

1594
01:08:55,633 --> 01:08:56,833
I'll see what's cooking.

1595
01:08:56,833 --> 01:08:59,333
"Sure. Swell. Palooka. 
Wise guy, huh?"

1596
01:08:59,333 --> 01:09:01,000
 It looks like we can't do a thing

1597
01:09:01,000 --> 01:09:02,233
until they move 
some more of the material.

1598
01:09:02,233 --> 01:09:05,000
That's right. Our beams 
are all transverse

1599
01:09:05,000 --> 01:09:06,200
on a horizontal plane.

1600
01:09:06,200 --> 01:09:07,467
"Uh, sir, your fly's..."

1601
01:09:07,467 --> 01:09:09,567
Anything would have to cross 
in front of these beams first

1602
01:09:09,567 --> 01:09:11,900
- "Uh, sir, your..." 
- before we get a reaction.

1603
01:09:11,900 --> 01:09:13,800
 But they may hole up for months.

1604
01:09:13,800 --> 01:09:15,900
Look, if we put 
our equipment up high,

1605
01:09:15,900 --> 01:09:17,800
we can spot them 
in no time, couldn't we?

1606
01:09:17,800 --> 01:09:20,100
You mean fly over \the city 
in a plane?

1607
01:09:20,100 --> 01:09:22,100
- Exactly. 
- "No, a duck."

1608
01:09:22,100 --> 01:09:24,133
 Even if you could throttle down 
 to a hundred miles an hour,

1609
01:09:24,133 --> 01:09:27,100
 I'm afraid that'd be too fast 
 for our outfit to pick up anything.

1610
01:09:27,100 --> 01:09:28,567
It doesn't have to be a plane.

1611
01:09:28,567 --> 01:09:29,933
No, how about a balloon?

1612
01:09:29,933 --> 01:09:31,300
 Sure, or a blimp or a helicopter.

1613
01:09:31,300 --> 01:09:33,100
Anything that'll float 
right over the rooftops.

1614
01:09:33,100 --> 01:09:34,400
And beam down on 
the hidden material?

1615
01:09:34,400 --> 01:09:35,433
That's right.

1616
01:09:35,433 --> 01:09:37,167
I'll get busy on it right away.

1617
01:09:37,167 --> 01:09:38,833
"See? Here I am. 
Busy, busy, busy."

1618
01:09:38,833 --> 01:09:41,233
Get me the airfield.

1619
01:09:41,233 --> 01:09:45,133
Man, she must get a keg 
of Cutty Sark every weekend.

1620
01:09:45,133 --> 01:09:48,400
Is that unusual?

1621
01:09:48,400 --> 01:09:50,533
Pretty close call, eh, Moran?

1622
01:09:50,533 --> 01:09:52,967
I can't understand how 
they could spot me so quickly.

1623
01:09:52,967 --> 01:09:55,567
( all whispering ) 
Radar! Radar!

1624
01:09:55,567 --> 01:09:57,867
We can't make a move 
without ringing a bell.

1625
01:09:57,867 --> 01:10:00,467
 - That's exactly what I have in mind. 
 - Ding, ding, ding.

1626
01:10:00,467 --> 01:10:02,767
Say we move another load 
of the material.

1627
01:10:02,767 --> 01:10:05,067
They send out an alert, 
and the chase is on.

1628
01:10:05,067 --> 01:10:06,267
Wait a minute. 
We just tried that deal.

1629
01:10:06,267 --> 01:10:07,567
Didn't pan out so good.

1630
01:10:07,567 --> 01:10:09,533
 Why don't you wait till Michael 
 tells you what he has in mind.

1631
01:10:09,533 --> 01:10:10,900
 "Oh, Michael this, Michael that.

1632
01:10:10,900 --> 01:10:12,367
Why don't you just marry him?"

1633
01:10:12,367 --> 01:10:15,100
- I'm listening. 
- As I started to say,

1634
01:10:15,100 --> 01:10:17,433
we load a couple 
of cartons into a car

1635
01:10:17,433 --> 01:10:18,633
and start it across town,

1636
01:10:18,633 --> 01:10:22,533
but in the opposite direction.

1637
01:10:22,533 --> 01:10:25,000
"'Budget Park and Ride.' 
This is ridiculous!"

1638
01:10:25,000 --> 01:10:27,200
They're on the moon.

1639
01:10:28,933 --> 01:10:31,667
- "Wash me." 
- ( chuckling )

1640
01:10:31,667 --> 01:10:34,400
 ♪ And on this farm he had a chick ♪

1641
01:10:34,400 --> 01:10:37,833
 ♪ The swingin'-est chick I know ♪

1642
01:10:37,833 --> 01:10:41,133
"Waldo Pepper, you in there?"

1643
01:10:41,133 --> 01:10:43,467
- Get any action? 
- ( scoffs ) In this movie?

1644
01:10:43,467 --> 01:10:45,467
Yeah, we're loading 
the stuff right now.

1645
01:10:45,467 --> 01:10:47,033
Who's loading 
what stuff right now?

1646
01:10:47,033 --> 01:10:48,633
Quit squawking. 
I'll tell you about it later.

1647
01:10:48,633 --> 01:10:51,133
Hey, Gus, Joe, come here.

1648
01:10:51,133 --> 01:10:53,200
 - These are the good guys, right? 
 - I don't know.

1649
01:10:53,200 --> 01:10:55,200
Get a couple small cartons 
off the back of the truck

1650
01:10:55,200 --> 01:10:57,333
and load them 
in the turtleback of my car.

1651
01:10:57,333 --> 01:10:59,233
Okay, boss.

1652
01:10:59,233 --> 01:11:02,767
I bet they're pushing 
counterfeit blue jeans.

1653
01:11:02,767 --> 01:11:05,033
We're gonna put 
some of the stuff in the car

1654
01:11:05,033 --> 01:11:07,400
to use as a decoy to keep 
the radar patrol off our tail.

1655
01:11:07,400 --> 01:11:09,800
 - "Oh, like a duck, sir." 
 - If we can get 'em on our tail.

1656
01:11:09,800 --> 01:11:11,867
We're not gonna be in the car.

1657
01:11:11,867 --> 01:11:13,267
We're gonna be in the truck 
with the rest of the stuff

1658
01:11:13,267 --> 01:11:14,867
on the way to the yacht.

1659
01:11:14,867 --> 01:11:17,500
- You get it? 
- Who is gonna be in the car?

1660
01:11:17,500 --> 01:11:20,133
"Ric Ocasek."

1661
01:11:21,167 --> 01:11:23,800
"Girl Scout cookie time."

1662
01:11:23,800 --> 01:11:26,800
"Each box contains 
a piece of Sid Melton."

1663
01:11:26,800 --> 01:11:30,000
 So where are we-- 
 In the trunk? We're in the thing?

1664
01:11:30,000 --> 01:11:32,633
 - In the yacht. 
 - They'll never be caught... alive.

1665
01:11:32,633 --> 01:11:33,667
( maniacal laughter )

1666
01:11:33,667 --> 01:11:36,800
Hey, don't cut away so fast.

1667
01:11:36,800 --> 01:11:39,500
"Barely room for 
Mr. Hoffa in there."

1668
01:11:42,400 --> 01:11:43,500
Now, here's the dope.

1669
01:11:43,500 --> 01:11:45,267
 "Take it to Kurt Cobain's house."

1670
01:11:45,267 --> 01:11:48,700
- Get him! Get him! 
- Might be able to...

1671
01:11:48,700 --> 01:11:50,500
Just take it easy 
and don't drive fast.

1672
01:11:50,500 --> 01:11:53,133
Get back as fast as you can. 
Key's in the car.

1673
01:11:53,133 --> 01:11:54,733
Right.

1674
01:12:06,567 --> 01:12:08,300
♪ This is a thing 
you've never known before ♪

1675
01:12:08,300 --> 01:12:09,600
- Servo! 
- ♪ It's called-- ♪ Aah!

1676
01:12:09,600 --> 01:12:10,867
- Aah! 
- Hey.

1677
01:12:10,867 --> 01:12:13,133
Say, Crow and I did 
a little project we want to do

1678
01:12:13,133 --> 01:12:14,633
 when we get back down to Earth, 
 and we were wondering...

1679
01:12:14,633 --> 01:12:16,900
What you got? 
A little project here?

1680
01:12:16,900 --> 01:12:19,000
Well, bring it on in here, 
you little scamps.

1681
01:12:19,000 --> 01:12:21,200
Let me have a look at it. 
Well, say.

1682
01:12:21,200 --> 01:12:23,667
 It's the Quinn Martin Nature Preserve.

1683
01:12:23,667 --> 01:12:26,867
 It's a place where old Quinn Martin 
 character actors will go

1684
01:12:26,867 --> 01:12:29,067
 to roam free in their natural habitat.

1685
01:12:29,067 --> 01:12:30,967
What the hell?

1686
01:12:30,967 --> 01:12:33,067
Yeah. Enter Q.M.N.P. here,

1687
01:12:33,067 --> 01:12:36,167
and pay a nominal fee 
to ticket taker Anthony Zerbe.

1688
01:12:36,167 --> 01:12:39,200
As you drive on, 
the rolling hills are dotted

1689
01:12:39,200 --> 01:12:42,733
 with the fauna of early Quinn Martin 
 success, The Untouchables.

1690
01:12:42,733 --> 01:12:45,533
Here, Robert Stack 
playfully pulls a muskrat

1691
01:12:45,533 --> 01:12:48,300
from the mouth 
of fellow G-man Jerry Paris,

